<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<odoo>
    <record id="view_immich_setup_wizard" model="ir.ui.view">
        <field name="name">immich.setup.wizard.form</field>
        <field name="model">immich.setup.wizard</field>
        <field name="arch" type="xml">
            <form string="Set up the photo server">
                <field name="step" widget="statusbar" readonly="1"/>
                <div invisible="step != 'intro'">
                    <p>Your guides' photos go to a photo server at the office; the website shows them online. You need:</p>
                    <ul>
                        <li>A NAS (or any always-on computer) at the office with <b>Docker</b>.</li>
                        <li><b>Immich</b> on it (the photo app guides and the office use).</li>
                        <li>A <b>storage bucket</b> for the photos shown online (Settings › Cloudflare: an R2 bucket).</li>
                    </ul>
                    <p>This wizard saves the office addresses, makes the photo server's token and gives you a zip with
                        its files and settings.</p>
                </div>
                <div invisible="step != 'network'">
                    <group>
                        <field name="lan_address" placeholder="192.168.1.50"/>
                        <field name="immich_port"/>
                        <field name="upload_port"/>
                        <field name="wifi_name"/>
                    </group>
                    <group string="Secure office address">
                        <field name="nas_domain" placeholder="example.com"/>
                        <field name="nas_hostname" readonly="1" class="font-monospace"/>
                        <field name="https_port" invisible="not nas_domain"/>
                    </group>
                    <p class="text-muted">Phones can only upload from the Staff App to an <b>https</b> address. The
                        secure office address gives the photo server one: it only works on the office network, so at
                        the office photos go straight from the phone to the photo server and never over the internet.
                        Leave the domain empty to skip it (uploads from the app then go over the internet).</p>
                </div>
                <div invisible="step != 'secure'">
                    <p>Two things make <b><field name="nas_hostname" readonly="1" class="oe_inline font-monospace"/></b>
                        work: a DNS record that points it to the photo server, and a certificate.</p>
                    <h5>1. The DNS record</h5>
                    <p class="mb-1">A public record that points to the photo server's office address (it answers
                        only on the office network). Add it in Cloudflare › your domain › DNS › Records:</p>
                    <table class="table table-sm w-auto font-monospace mb-2">
                        <tr><th>Type</th><th>Name</th><th>IPv4 address</th><th>Proxy status</th></tr>
                        <tr><td>A</td><td><field name="nas_hostname" readonly="1" class="oe_inline"/></td>
                            <td><field name="lan_address" readonly="1" class="oe_inline"/></td>
                            <td>DNS only (grey cloud)</td></tr>
                    </table>
                    <div invisible="not can_create_dns" class="mb-2">
                        <button name="action_dns_create" type="object" string="Create the record" icon="fa-globe"
                                class="btn-secondary"/>
                        <span class="text-muted small ms-2">With the token below, or the Cloudflare connector's.</span>
                    </div>
                    <field name="can_create_dns" invisible="1"/>
                    <field name="dns_result" readonly="1" invisible="not dns_result" nolabel="1"/>
                    <h5 class="mt-3">2. The certificate</h5>
                    <p class="mb-1">The photo server gets its own certificate (Let's Encrypt) by proving the domain
                        through Cloudflare, so it needs a Cloudflare API token that can edit this domain's DNS:
                        <a href="https://dash.cloudflare.com/profile/api-tokens" target="_blank">Cloudflare › My
                        Profile › API Tokens</a> › Create Token › "Edit zone DNS" template, Zone Resources: only this
                        domain. Paste it here: it goes only into the zip's <code>tls/tls.env</code> and leaves this
                        wizard on Finish.</p>
                    <group>
                        <field name="dns_token" password="True" placeholder="Cloudflare API token"/>
                    </group>
                    <h5 class="mt-3">3. Check</h5>
                    <button name="action_resolve" type="object" string="Check the name" icon="fa-search"
                            class="btn-secondary"/>
                    <field name="resolve_result" readonly="1" invisible="not resolve_result" nolabel="1"/>
                    <p class="text-muted small mt-2">This checks from here that the name points to the photo server.
                        The https check happens on a phone at the office, on the last step.</p>
                </div>
                <div invisible="step != 'token'">
                    <p>The photo server signs every call with this token. Making a new one stops the old one.</p>
                    <button name="action_token" type="object" string="Make the token" icon="fa-key"
                            class="btn-primary" invisible="token"/>
                    <div invisible="not token">
                        <p class="mb-1">Shown once: it goes into the zip of the next step.</p>
                        <field name="token" readonly="1" class="font-monospace"/>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <div invisible="step != 'files'">
                    <p>Download the photo server's files: <code>config.env</code> (this site's address, the token, the
                        bucket, the secure office address and its certificate token; fill in the blank secrets),
                        <code>docker-compose.yml</code>, the sync service, the https front and <code>README.md</code>
                        with the install steps. Keep the zip private.</p>
                    <button name="action_download" type="object" string="Download the zip" icon="fa-download"
                            class="btn-primary"/>
                </div>
                <div invisible="step != 'check'">
                    <p>Install the files on the photo server and start it. Waiting for the photo server to call in…</p>
                    <group>
                        <field name="last_seen" readonly="1"/>
                    </group>
                    <p invisible="last_seen" class="text-muted">No call yet.</p>
                    <button name="action_check" type="object" string="Check again" icon="fa-refresh"/>
                    <div invisible="not ping_url" class="mt-3">
                        <p class="mb-1">Then, on a phone on the office wifi, open
                            <field name="ping_url" widget="url" readonly="1" class="oe_inline"/>: it must say
                            <code>{"ok":true}</code>. Finish makes it the office upload page.</p>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <footer>
                    <button name="action_back" type="object" string="Back" invisible="step == 'intro'"/>
                    <button name="action_next" type="object" string="Next" class="btn-primary"
                            invisible="step == 'check'"/>
                    <button name="action_finish" type="object" string="Finish" class="btn-primary"
                            invisible="step != 'check'"/>
                    <button string="Cancel" special="cancel" invisible="step == 'check'"/>
                </footer>
            </form>
        </field>
    </record>

    <record id="action_immich_setup_wizard" model="ir.actions.act_window">
        <field name="name">Set up the photo server</field>
        <field name="res_model">immich.setup.wizard</field>
        <field name="view_mode">form</field>
        <field name="target">new</field>
    </record>
</odoo>
